add([(1892,'statement','SLAM definition and core functions: occupancy map, pose and loop closure.')],'slam','Joint map and pose estimation','Which task distinguishes SLAM from localization in an already supplied map?',['Jointly estimating the robot pose and constructing/updating a map of initially unknown surroundings.','Estimating pose while treating a prebuilt map as fixed input.','Following a supplied path without updating pose or map.','Converting desired velocity into motor PWM alone.'],'SLAM estimates pose and map together; the source specifically describes an unknown environment.',['This describes map-based localization rather than map construction.','Path following is a control task, not joint estimation.','Motor control does not solve the mapping problem.'])
add([(1892,'statement','Loop closure corrects accumulated localization errors; source uses a2D occupancy grid.')],'loop','Loop closure and map scope','Which statement matches this lecture’s SLAM description without generalizing beyond it?',['Its2D occupancy-map workflow uses loop closure to constrain accumulated pose/map error when revisiting a place.','Every SLAM system is limited to2D occupancy grids and cannot estimate3D structure.','Loop closure means commanding the robot to drive a geometrically perfect circle.','Loop closure is the same operation as clearing a wheel-speed integral.'],'Here loop closure means recognizing/reconciling a revisited place to constrain accumulated estimation error; the2D representation is this example’s choice.',['The lecture’s representation is not a universal restriction on SLAM.','A revisited place need not lie on a circular path.','PID integral handling is a different control operation.'])
add([(1893,'statement','Teleoperate, acquire scans, filtered odometry, update/save map; outputs for AMCL/Nav2.')],'map-workflow','Mapping and later reuse','Which sequence matches the demonstrated LiDAR mapping workflow?',['Teleoperate while collecting scans and filtered odometry, build/update the grid, then save it for localization and navigation.','Load a completed map, disable scans, and infer every new wall from wheel counts alone.','Run the object detector once and use its class list as the occupancy grid.','Save motor PWM values and use them directly as AMCL’s spatial map.'],'The source mapping procedure combines sensor scans and pose estimates and saves the resulting spatial map.',['Unknown surroundings are not reconstructed from wheel counts alone.','Class labels do not constitute the spatial occupancy grid.','Motor commands are not a map representation.'])
add([(1893,'figure','Initial LiDAR scan screenshot and completed2D occupancy-grid screenshot.')],'map-images','Scan versus accumulated map','The initial scan view and completed grid view on page1893 represent which distinction?',['A sensor observation around a current pose versus an environment representation accumulated through mapping.','Two independently verified ground-truth maps with stated error bounds.','A wheel encoder waveform versus a camera calibration pattern.','The same raw image merely recoloured by a classifier.'],'The figures illustrate an initial scan and a completed map; they are not accompanied by accuracy or timing measurements.',['No ground-truth error bounds are printed.','Neither figure is an encoder waveform or calibration target.','Mapping combines geometry over motion, not merely recolouring an image.'],typ='diagram')
add([(1894,'statement','AMCL prebuilt map, particle filter and multiple pose hypotheses.')],'amcl','AMCL hypothesis representation','What do AMCL particles represent in the source account?',['Alternative hypotheses for robot pose in a prebuilt map.','Individual laser photons stored as permanent map cells.','Motor PWM levels selected for the next wheel update.','Object class labels replacing spatial pose estimates.'],'A particle filter represents multiple candidate poses and changes their probabilities with observations.',['Particles here are state hypotheses, not physical photons.','They are not the actuator command set.','Semantic classes and pose hypotheses are different variables.'])
WF=formula('wi+=wi− ℓi / Σj(wj− ℓj)',{'wi−,wi+':'prior and normalized posterior probability weight of pose hypothesis i','ℓi':'likelihood of the current observation under hypothesis i','i,j':'hypothesis indices'},{'wi−,wi+':'dimensionless probabilities','ℓi':'common likelihood units cancel in normalization','i,j':'dimensionless indices'},['Added discrete hypothesis example; finite positive normalization, likelihoods proportional to the same observation density; no resampling specified.'])
add([(1894,'figure','Scan comparison → pose probability update → best robot pose.')],'particles','Added particle-weight calculation','Added three-pose example: prior weights(0.5,0.3,0.2) and scan likelihoods(0.2,0.5,0.5). Normalize the products. Which result is correct?',['Posterior weights(2/7,3/7,2/7); the second hypothesis has largest weight.','Posterior weights(0.5,0.3,0.2); the first hypothesis remains largest.','Posterior weights(1/6,5/12,5/12); the second and third tie.','Posterior weights(0.1,0.15,0.1); the second is largest and the list is already normalized.'],'Products are0.10,0.15,0.10 with sum0.35, giving2/7,3/7,2/7.',['This ignores the observation likelihoods.','This normalizes likelihoods alone and discards prior weights.','The products sum to0.35, not1, so normalization is still needed.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Added finite hypothesis model; weights update by prior×likelihood. No claim of exact source AMCL configuration.'],steps=['Multiply each prior by its corresponding likelihood.','Sum0.10+0.15+0.10=0.35.','Divide each product by0.35.'],forms=[WF])
add([(1894,'statement','Multiple pose hypotheses and localization confidence.')],'ambiguity','Ambiguous localization','Two symmetric corridors yield similar scan agreement for two separated particle clusters. What is the defensible interpretation?',['The localization belief can remain ambiguous; a single displayed pose need not imply high certainty.','The two clusters automatically prove the map is metrically wrong.','The cluster with the larger x coordinate must be the true pose.','AMCL must average the two corridors into a physically traversable new corridor.'],'Multiple hypotheses represent ambiguity; additional informative observations or movement may be needed to distinguish them.',['Ambiguity can arise in a correct symmetric map.','Coordinate magnitude is not an observation likelihood.','A pose distribution does not create a new physical corridor.'],typ='scenario',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Added symmetric-map example; no unique disambiguating observation yet.'])
add([(1895,'table','Goal pose parameter table x,y,theta and goal-arrow screenshot.')],'goal','Goal pose and orientation','A planar goal specifies the correct x and y but an incorrect final heading. Which conclusion follows from the source definition?',['It is not the same goal pose, because desired orientation is part of the specification.','It is the same pose because pose contains position only.','The heading is irrelevant whenever the global path has equal length.','The goal arrow specifies speed rather than orientation.'],'The goal includes x, y and desired orientation θ.',['Position is only part of a pose.','Route length does not erase an orientation requirement.','The screenshot’s directed goal arrow encodes orientation, not a velocity value.'])
add([(1895,'figure','RViz toolbar shows2D Pose Estimate separately from2D Goal Pose.')],'goal-tools','Initial estimate versus destination','Which distinction is important when reading the two RViz tools shown in the screenshot?',['A pose estimate initializes where the robot is believed to be; a goal pose specifies where it should go.','Both tools specify motor PWM but at different frequencies.','A goal pose edits the LiDAR calibration; a pose estimate changes battery capacity.','Both tools change only the camera’s image crop.'],'Current-pose belief and desired destination have distinct roles.',['Neither tool is a PWM setting.','These are unrelated hardware settings.','The tools act on robot navigation poses, not image crops.'],typ='diagram')
add([(1896,'statement','Map and robot pose+goal inputs; static obstacles and replanning.')],'global','Global route planning','What is the global planner’s principal source-described output?',['A long-range route from the current pose toward the goal using map obstacle information.','The motor driver’s current waveform for each PWM period.','An RGB bounding box and object confidence score.','A newly measured encoder count for each wheel.'],'The global planner provides a map-based route; execution and sensing are separate stages.',['Current regulation is lower-level drive control.','These are perception outputs.','Counts are measurements, not global planning output.'])
add([(1896,'statement','Optimal collision-free path and smooth efficient routes claims.','conflict')],'optimal-boundary','Meaning of optimal planning','The slide calls the global path optimal and collision-free but gives no algorithm, objective or robot footprint. Which reading is justified?',['These describe planning aims; proving them for an actual run requires a defined cost, map/footprint and algorithm assumptions.','Optimal necessarily means the fewest cells, regardless of clearance penalties.','A collision-free centerline proves every finite robot body fits along it.','A path optimal in an outdated map remains collision-free after a new obstacle appears.'],'Optimality and collision claims are conditional on a specified problem and valid environment model.',['The cost function may include other terms.','Finite footprint and clearance must be checked.','Environmental change may invalidate the path and motivate replanning.'],conflict='p1896 labels paths optimal, collision-free and smooth without giving the optimization objective or collision model. Preserve the claim as an objective, not an unconditional proof.')
CF=formula('J=Lpath+λC',{'J':'added total route cost','Lpath':'route length','C':'dimensionless accumulated obstacle-proximity penalty','λ':'penalty weight'},{'J,Lpath':'m-equivalent cost','C':'dimensionless','λ':'m per penalty unit'},['Added illustrative cost; infeasible/colliding candidates are excluded first. No claim this is the source planner implementation.'])
add([(1896,'statement','Global planning uses map obstacles and efficient routes.')],'route-cost','Cost-defined route choice','Added planner rule: exclude colliding paths, then minimize J=length+0.5×penalty. Candidate A is feasible with length8 m and penalty6; B is feasible with length10 m and penalty0; C collides with length6 m and penalty0. Which is selected?',['B.','A.','C.','A and B tie.'],'A costs11, B costs10, and C is excluded for collision, so B is selected.',['A is shorter but its penalty makes total cost larger.','A colliding path is infeasible before cost comparison.','The costs11 and10 do not tie.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Added cost and candidate data, not source runtime output. Collision is a hard constraint.'],steps=['Discard C.','Compute JA=8+0.5(6)=11 and JB=10.','Choose the feasible lower cost.'],forms=[CF])
add([(1897,'statement','Local planning reacts to new obstacles and generates feasible velocity commands.')],'local','Local planning response','A new nearby obstacle was absent from the saved map. Which source-described function reacts while tracking the global route?',['Local planning/control using current obstacle information to generate feasible velocity commands.','The saved map file by itself, without new sensor processing.','The wheel-radius constant, without any trajectory update.','The object class name alone, without obstacle geometry or control.'],'Local planning reacts to newly detected obstacles while pursuing the global route.',['A static file cannot detect a new obstacle alone.','Geometry parameters do not implement obstacle-aware command updates.','Semantic labels alone are insufficient for geometric motion control.'],typ='scenario')
add([(1896,'statement','Global path is long-range and can be replanned.'),(1897,'statement','Local planner follows global path, evaluates trajectories and sends velocity commands.')],'plan-split','Global versus local planning','Which division of responsibility is most consistent with both pages?',['Global planning selects the longer-range route; local planning tracks it while responding to nearby obstacles and motion feasibility.','Global planning computes every motor PWM sample; local planning builds the entire map from scratch.','Local planning fixes one route forever; global planning responds only to encoder interrupts.','Global planning and local planning both mean YOLO object classification.'],'The two levels connect route selection with short-horizon feasible motion.',['This assigns drive-level and mapping work to the wrong layers.','The source explicitly permits global replanning and local response.','Perception is distinct from planning.'])
add([(1898,'table','Topic asynchronous communication; service request-response; action long-running tasks; TF transforms.')],'ros-comms','ROS communication choices','Which source-table pairing is appropriate for repeated sensor messages, a bounded request-response operation, and a long-running navigation goal?',['Topic; service; action.','Service; action; topic.','Action; topic; service.','TF; topic; node.'],'Topics carry asynchronous streams, services handle request-response and actions suit longer tasks such as navigation.',['This permutes all three roles.','This likewise assigns long tasks and streams to the wrong abstractions.','TF and node are not the requested three communication roles.'])
add([(1898,'figure','Sensor box→Processing Nodes→Navigation and Motor Controller→Mobile Robot.')],'node-flow','Processing and control diagram','What does the node-architecture figure show processing nodes receiving?',['Camera, LiDAR, IMU and encoder information.','Only the final target pose with all sensor paths omitted.','Only battery current, with no perception input.','Only the output of the motors after the robot reaches its goal.'],'The sensor box explicitly lists camera, LiDAR, IMU and encoder inputs to processing nodes.',['The figure includes the sensor path.','The listed inputs are not a battery-only channel.','Information feeds processing during operation, not only after goal completion.'],typ='diagram')
add([(1898,'table','Node row calls it an independent executable performing a specific task.')],'node-evidence','Node diagram evidence limit','The slide calls a node an independent executable, but supplies no launch or process configuration. What can be concluded directly from this slide?',['It describes task-oriented processing components; it does not document the actual runtime process layout of this robot.','It documents the exact operating-system process ID of every node.','It specifies the exact topic names and message schemas for every sensor.','It proves all processing runs as one fixed executable on the Arduino.'],'The table is a conceptual introduction; actual launch/process and message configuration is not printed.',['No process IDs are supplied.','No topic or message definitions are supplied.','The source does not establish that process layout and elsewhere assigns high-level processing to Jetson.'],assumptions=['Question concerns evidence provided by the page, not an unverified reconstruction of a ROS launch system.'])
add([(1899,'table','Planner Server, Controller Server, BT Navigator, Costmaps and Recovery Behaviors roles.')],'nav2-servers','Nav2 server responsibilities','Which assignment matches the Nav2 table?',['Planner Server computes a global path; Controller Server tracks it; BT Navigator coordinates navigation behavior.','Planner Server counts encoder edges; Controller Server classifies camera objects; BT Navigator regulates battery voltage.','Planner Server is the RGB camera driver; Controller Server saves images; BT Navigator builds wheel geometry.','Planner Server and Controller Server are both names for AMCL pose particles.'],'The table separates global route generation, trajectory tracking and navigation behavior.',['These sensor/drive roles do not match the servers.','The servers are not those acquisition/modeling functions.','AMCL is a localization component, not a synonym for either planning server.'])
add([(1899,'table','Costmaps maintain obstacle information; recovery behaviors recover from navigation failures.')],'nav2-recovery','Recovery versus normal planning','Which pair follows the source table?',['Costmaps maintain obstacle information; recovery behaviors address navigation failures.','Costmaps store only object class names; recovery behaviors define encoder counts per revolution.','Costmaps calibrate IMU bias; recovery behaviors set the camera focal length.','Costmaps are motor power stages; recovery behaviors are wheel encoders.'],'The table gives obstacle representation and failure-handling roles respectively.',['This replaces spatial obstacle information and recovery with unrelated tasks.','Calibration functions are not the stated pair.','These are software navigation concepts, not the listed hardware devices.'])
add([(1899,'statement','Implementation bullets: SLAM-generated map, AMCL, filtered odometry, LiDAR+RGB-D.')],'nav-inputs','Navigation information inputs','Which set matches the reported Nav2 implementation inputs?',['SLAM-generated occupancy map, AMCL localization, EKF-filtered odometry, and LiDAR/RGB-D obstacle information.','Only a camera class label and a battery voltage, with no pose or obstacle geometry.','Only a fixed wheel-speed sequence, without a map or localization.','Only a target pixel coordinate, with no spatial calibration or robot pose.'],'The source explicitly lists map, localization, filtered odometry and multisensor obstacles.',['These inputs omit the reported spatial and state information.','The reported system is not a purely fixed open-loop sequence.','A pixel coordinate alone is not the full navigation input set.'])
add([(1900,'statement','Detect RGB objects; use aligned depth; publish annotated images; Jetson processing.')],'yolo','Detection and distance','Which statement correctly separates YOLO detection from the distance stage described here?',['RGB detection provides boxes/classes/scores; aligned depth supplies metric distance information.','The confidence score is the object distance in metres.','A bounding-box width alone is the exact3D range without a size/calibration model.','An object class label supplies the robot’s map-frame pose.'],'The source explicitly adds aligned depth for distance; detection semantics alone do not provide metric range.',['Confidence is not a distance unit.','Image size alone requires additional assumptions to infer range.','Object classification is not robot localization.'])
add([(1900,'figure','Detection photograph with chair bounding box labelled0.83; network outputs boxes,class,confidence.')],'confidence','Detection score interpretation','The screenshot labels a chair detection with score0.83. What can be inferred without a calibration study?',['It is the displayed detector score for that detection, not a proven83% correctness frequency in deployment.','The chair is0.83 m from the camera.','The bounding box covers83% of the image area.','The complete navigation system succeeds on83% of runs.'],'The score is a detector output; the slide provides no statistical calibration or task-success evaluation.',['Metric distance comes from depth, not this label.','A confidence score is not a box-area fraction.','One detection score does not measure end-to-end success rate.'],typ='diagram')
ZF=formula('Z=s d; X=(u−cx)Z/fx; Y=(v−cy)Z/fy',{'Z':'optical-axis depth','X,Y':'camera-frame lateral coordinates','d':'raw valid depth value registered to the selected colour pixel','s':'depth scale','u,v':'selected colour pixel coordinates','cx,cy':'principal point coordinates','fx,fy':'focal lengths in pixel units'},{'X,Y,Z':'m','d':'raw depth units','s':'m per raw unit','u,v,cx,cy,fx,fy':'pixels'},['Added pinhole/calibration example with aligned depth, corrected distortion and matching pixels; Z is axial depth, not Euclidean range.'])
add([(1900,'statement','Object distance uses aligned depth data.')],'depth-number','Aligned depth and camera point','Added calibrated example: aligned raw depth d=1500 with scale0.001 m/unit, fx=fy=500 pixels, principal point(320,240), selected pixel(420,240). What camera point follows the supplied pinhole rule?',['(0.30,0,1.50) m.','(0,0,1.50) m.','(0.30,0,1500) m.','(0.20,0,1.50) m.'],'Z=1.5 m, X=(420−320)1.5/500=0.30 m and Y=0.',['This ignores the horizontal pixel offset.','This fails to apply the depth scale to Z.','This reports normalized image x without multiplying by depth.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['All calibration/depth values and pinhole rule are added; valid registered depth and corrected distortion. No actual camera execution.'],steps=['Convert raw depth to metres.','Subtract principal point and divide by focal length.','Multiply normalized coordinates by Z.'],forms=[ZF])
add([(1900,'statement','Aligned depth used for object distance.')],'alignment','RGB-depth correspondence','RGB and depth images have different viewpoints and are not registered. Why is reading depth at the same integer pixel index unsafe?',['The indices may refer to different rays/objects, so registration or an explicit geometric correspondence is needed.','Identical array indices guarantee identical world points regardless of camera geometry.','Changing the object class name aligns the two images.','A higher detector confidence corrects any depth-colour extrinsic error.'],'The source’s alignment requirement matters because geometry, not index equality alone, establishes correspondence.',['Different sensor geometries invalidate this assumption.','Labels do not geometrically register images.','Confidence does not repair calibration.'],typ='scenario',difficulty='medium')
add([(1901,'statement','Virtual robot/environment, same ROS2 stack and realistic sensor outputs.')],'simulation','Why simulate before deployment','What is a supported advantage of the described simulation stage?',['Testing mapping, localization, planning and control with modeled sensors before deploying the same high-level stack to hardware.','Proving the physical robot has no unmodeled friction or sensor latency.','Eliminating the need to check real sensor calibration.','Establishing hardware safety solely from a rendered animation.'],'Simulation supplies a controlled environment for integration testing, while physical validation still addresses model mismatch.',['A model does not prove absence of unmodeled effects.','Physical calibration remains relevant.','Animation alone is not a safety demonstration.'])
