add([(1903,'figure','Speed4x label beneath map-generation frame.'),(1904,'figure','Speed3x label beneath simulation-video frame.'),(1905,'figure','Speed3.25x label beside hardware-video frame.')],'playback','Playback speed versus experiment time','Added timing question: a real-world recording is played at3.25x and occupies20 s of playback. Assuming constant playback scaling, what original duration does it represent?',['65 s.','6.15 s approximately.','23.25 s.','20 s.'],'Original duration is playback duration times speed factor:20×3.25=65 s.',['This divides instead of multiplying.','Playback scaling is multiplicative, not additive.','That would require1x playback.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='easy',assumptions=['Added20 s playback duration; constant multiplier, no edits or pauses. It does not infer physical robot velocity.'],steps=['Original duration=multiplication factor×displayed duration.'],forms=[formula('toriginal=k tplayback',{'toriginal':'original recording interval','tplayback':'elapsed playback interval','k':'playback acceleration factor'},{'toriginal,tplayback':'s','k':'dimensionless'},['Constant scaling without edits or pauses; factor is not m/s.'])])
# Integrated exercises use source architecture as premise, with every new numerical/model assumption stated.
ODF=formula('Δθ=(ΔsR−ΔsL)/L; Δs=(ΔsR+ΔsL)/2; Δx=ρ[sin(θ+Δθ)−sinθ]; Δy=−ρ[cos(θ+Δθ)−cosθ]; ρ=Δs/Δθ',{'ΔsR,ΔsL':'right and left signed wheel travels','L':'full wheel separation','Δs':'midpoint traveled arc length','θ':'initial heading in the fixed frame','Δθ':'heading change','ρ':'signed turn radius','Δx,Δy':'fixed-frame displacement increments'},{'ΔsR,ΔsL,L,Δs,ρ,Δx,Δy':'m','θ,Δθ':'rad'},['Added exact constant-curvature integration for ideal planar rolling and Δθ≠0; zero-turn limit uses Δx=Δs cosθ and Δy=Δs sinθ.'])
add([DREF,(1887,'statement','Encoder counts → wheel displacement → pose using differential-drive kinematics.')],'odom-arc','Integrated encoder arc','Added ideal example: r=1/(2π) m, N=1000 decoded counts/revolution, ΔnR=200, ΔnL=100 and L=0.50 m. Initial pose is(0,0,0). Assume constant curvature and use exact arc integration. Which final pose is closest?',['x=0.1490 m, y=0.01495 m, θ=0.20 rad.','x=0.1500 m, y=0 m, θ=0.20 rad.','x=0.1490 m, y=−0.01495 m, θ=−0.20 rad.','x=0.2980 m, y=0.02990 m, θ=0.20 rad.'],'Travel is0.20 m right and0.10 m left. Δθ=0.20, midpoint arc0.15, radius0.75. Thus x=0.75sin0.2≈0.1490 and y=0.75(1−cos0.2)≈0.01495.',['This is a straight-heading Euler approximation, not the requested exact arc.','This reverses the right-minus-left yaw convention.','This doubles the midpoint arc length by omitting the average.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='hard',assumptions=['All values and exact-integration rule are added. Equal-radius no-slip motion; counts already include decoding/gearing.'],steps=['2πr=1 m/revolution, so travel values are0.20 and0.10 m.','Compute Δθ=0.10/0.50=0.20 and Δs=0.15.','Use radius0.75 and exact arc equations.'],forms=[EF,DF,ODF])
add([DREF,(1897,'statement','Generate feasible linear/angular velocity commands.')],'feasible-command','Wheel limits and commanded motion','Added drive limits: |ωR|,|ωL|≤4 rad/s, r=0.10 m and L=0.40 m. Requested(v,ω)=(0.30 m/s,1 rad/s). Which uniform scaling preserves curvature while meeting both wheel limits?',['Scale both body rates by0.8, giving(v,ω)=(0.24 m/s,0.8 rad/s).','Leave v unchanged and reduce only ω to0.5 rad/s.','Clip only the right wheel from5 to4 rad/s and leave the left at1 rad/s.','Increase both body rates by1.25.'],'Requested wheel rates are5 and1; common scale4/5 gives4 and0.8. Both body rates scale equally, preserving v/ω.',['This meets different wheel commands but changes curvature.','Unequal wheel scaling changes the ratio of body translation to yaw.','This worsens the exceeded wheel limit.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Added symmetric angular-rate limits; zero acceleration/transient constraints in this calculation. Uniform scaling is specifically requested.'],steps=['Compute requested wheel rates5 and1.','Set scale=min(1,4/max(5,1))=0.8.','Multiply both v and ω by0.8.'],forms=[IF,DF])
add([(1891,'figure','Costmap bands and finite-clearance obstacle representation.'),(1897,'statement','Feasible motion commands and nearby obstacle avoidance.')],'corridor','Footprint and corridor decision','Added simplified geometry: circular robot radius0.25 m, required margin0.10 m on each side, straight corridor width0.65 m. Which conclusion follows before choosing a centerline?',['The width is insufficient because required clearance is0.70 m.','It fits because the physical diameter0.50 m is below0.65 m and margins can be ignored.','It fits because the margin is added only once, requiring0.60 m.','It fits because any centerline in free cells certifies footprint clearance.'],'The required total width is2(r+m)=2(0.25+0.10)=0.70 m, greater than the corridor width.',['The explicit safety margins must also be respected.','A margin is required on each side.','Centerline cell occupancy alone omits finite footprint extent.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Added circular footprint, straight parallel walls and deterministic two-sided margin. No source robot dimensions are inferred.'],steps=['Add radius and per-side margin.','Double to get total required width.','Compare0.70 m with0.65 m.'],forms=[formula('Wrequired=2(rbody+m)',{'Wrequired':'minimum corridor width in the added circular-footprint model','rbody':'robot footprint radius','m':'required clearance per side'},{'Wrequired,rbody,m':'m'},['Straight parallel corridor; circular footprint; margin required on both sides.'])])
add([(1886,'figure','Hardware roles: battery, Jetson, Arduino, drivers, encoders and sensors.'),(1888,'figure','Encoder-feedback motor speed regulation.'),(1900,'statement','RGB/depth semantic perception on Jetson.')],'diagnosis','Cross-layer fault isolation','Added diagnostic observation: object annotations and maps update normally, but measured wheel speed drops under load while a velocity command remains constant. Which first investigation is best matched to the demonstrated architecture?',['Inspect encoder feedback, low-level PID/drive limits and motor power delivery rather than first retraining object labels.','Replace the saved map because any wheel-speed error is a map-origin error.','Change YOLO class names because they directly define motor current.','Change only the RViz colour palette because cost colours determine electrical torque.'],'The symptom concerns speed regulation and actuation under load; functioning perception does not rule out a drive/control/power issue.',['A constant local wheel-speed tracking error does not by itself identify map-origin error.','Class names are not motor-current commands.','Visualization colours are not electrical actuation settings.'],typ='scenario',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['Added diagnostic evidence; the suggested checks are hypotheses, not a claimed confirmed hardware fault.'])
